{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":1521688,"ns":0,"title":"TDK","revisions":[{"revid":1381749944,"parentid":1380082777,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-10-24T15:19:51Z","size":24796,"sha1":"f853eea6824d135496362f47c7f834c8281a6eea","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{short description|Japanese multinational electronics corporation}}\n{{about|the Japanese electronics company}}\n{{Use dmy dates|date=April 2022}}\n{{Infobox company\n| name                = TDK Corporation\n| logo                = TDK-Logo.svg\n| logo_class          = skin-invert\n| native_name         = TDK株式会社\n| native_name_lang    = ja\n| romanized_name      = TDK Kabushiki-gaisha\n| former_name         = TDK Electronics Co., Ltd (1935-1983)\n| type                = [[Public Company|Public]] ([[Kabushiki gaisha|K.K]])\n| traded_as           = {{Tyo|6762}}\n| founded             = {{Start date and age|df=yes|1935|12|07}} as ''Tokyo Denki Kagaku Kōgyō K.K.''\n| founder             = Kenzo Saito<ref>{{Cite web |title=Corporate Philosophy |url=https://www.tdk.com/en/about_tdk/code_of_conduct/chapter_one.html |access-date=2021-11-14 |website=TDK |language=en}}</ref>\n| hq_location         = [[Nihonbashi, Chūō, Tokyo]]\n| hq_location_city    =\n| hq_location_country = Japan\n| area_served         = Worldwide\n| key_people          = Noboru Saito ([[CEO (corporate title)|CEO]])\n| industry            = [[Electronics industry|Electronics]]\n| products            = Currently: [[Electronic component]]s, [[Power supply|Power supplies]], [[Sensor]]s<br />Previously: [[Compact cassette|Cassettes]], [[Compact disc|CDs]], [[DVDs]], [[Blu-ray Discs]]\n| revenue             = {{profit}} [[United States dollar|US$]]13.56 billion<br />(2021)<ref name=\"FY-March-2018\">{{cite web|url=https://www.global.tdk.com/ir/ir_library/financial/pdf/2018042700_6fce61bc.pdf |title=Financial Results for FY March 2018 |author=TDK Corporation |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181128075253/https://www.global.tdk.com/ir/ir_library/financial/pdf/2018042700_6fce61bc.pdf|archive-date=2018-11-28}}</ref>\n| operating_income    = {{profit}} US$1.15 Billion<br />(2021)<ref name=\"FY-March-2018\" />\n| net_income          = {{loss}} US$528.7 million<br />(2021)<ref name=\"FY-March-2018\" />\n| assets              = {{profit}} US$21.4 billion<br />(2021)<ref name=\"FY-March-2018\" />\n| equity              = {{profit}} JP¥831.2 billion<br />{{profit}} US$7.84 billion<br />(2018)<ref name=\"FY-March-2018\" />\n| num_employees       = 102,883<ref name=\"FY-March-2018\" />\n| parent              = \n| subsidiaries        = \n| website             = {{URL|https://www.tdk.com/}} \n}}\n{{Nihongo|'''TDK Corporation'''|TDK株式会社|TDK Kabushiki-gaisha|lead=yes}} is a Japanese [[multinational corporation|multinational]] [[electronics]] corporation that manufactures [[electronic component]]s and recording and [[Data storage device|data-storage media]]. Its motto is \"Contribute to culture and industry through creativity\".<ref>{{cite web|title=TDK Japan site: motto and principles.|url=http://www.tdk.co.jp/teaaa01/aaa04000.htm|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060823091744/http://www.tdk.co.jp/teaaa01/aaa04000.htm|archive-date=2006-08-23}}</ref>\n\n\"TDK\" is an initialism of the original Japanese name of the company: '''T'''okyo '''D'''enki '''K'''agaku Kōgyō K.K. (Tokyo Electric Chemical Industry Co., Ltd.). The company is listed on the [[Tokyo Stock Exchange]] and is a constituent of the [[Nikkei 225]] and [[TOPIX]] indices.\n\n==History==\n[[File:TDK本社.JPG|thumb|Former headquarters in [[Tokyo]]]]\n[[File:TDK Corporation of America Headquarters Cypress California 2021.JPG|thumb|TDK Corporation of America headquarters in [[Cypress, California]]]]\n[[File:Compactcassette.jpg|thumb|A TDK SA-90 [[compact cassette]]]]\nTDK was founded by Kenzo Saito in Tokyo, Japan, on 7 December 1935 to manufacture the [[iron]]-based magnetic material [[Ferrite (magnet)|ferrite]], which had been recently invented by Yogoro Kato and Takeshi Takei.<ref>{{cite web|title=TDK Hong Kong site |url=http://www.tdk.com.hk/english/profile/profile.htm |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060821042738/http://www.tdk.com.hk/english/profile/profile.htm |archive-date=2006-08-21 }}</ref> In 1952 and 1957, they began to produce magnetic tape, with [[compact cassette]] tapes following in 1966. TDK manufactured an extensive portfolio of magnetic and optical media, including several formats of [[videotape]] and blank [[CD-R]] and [[DVD recordable|recordable DVD]] discs until the recording media business was sold to [[Imation]] in 2007.<ref name='reg_sale' /><ref name='tdk_sale_pr' />\n\nTDK produced five million ferrite cores through 1945 that were primarily used to reduce the volume and weight of radio receivers used by the Imperial Japanese military.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5360693 |title= The Invention of Ferrites and Their Contribution to the Miniaturization of Radios |last1=Okamoto |first1=Akira |date=4 December 2009 |website=[[IEEE]] |pages= 1–6 |publisher=IEEE |doi= 10.1109/GLOCOMW.2009.5360693 |isbn= 978-1-4244-5626-0 |access-date=15 October 2020}}</ref>\n\nOperations in the United States began in 1965 with a New York City office,<ref name=\"corp history aae10000\" /> and European operations began in 1970 with an office in [[Frankfurt]], [[West Germany]].<ref>{{cite web|title=TDK Europe site: corporate history|url=http://www.tdk-europe.com/en/corporate/history.php|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071017050300/http://www.tdk-europe.com/en/corporate/history.php|archive-date=2007-10-17}}</ref>\n\nIn 1980, TDK developed a multilayering technology to create chip capacitors and inductors used in personal computers, laptops, smartphones and other electronic devices.{{citation needed|date=June 2026}}\n\nIn 1986, TDK acquired SAE Magnetics and introduced high-density recording heads.<ref name=\"tdk our history\">{{cite web|title=TDK: Our History|url=https://www.global.tdk.com/corp/en/about_tdk/our_history/index.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181128075157/https://www.global.tdk.com/corp/en/about_tdk/our_history/index.htm|archive-date=2018-11-28}}</ref>\n\nIn the 1990s, TDK's Mass Storage Division included [[Brushless DC electric motor|brushless DC spindle motors]], [[magnetoresistance]] (MR) heads and thin-film heads.{{citation needed|date=June 2026}}\n\nSince 1997, TDK has gradually withdrawn from the production of compact cassettes. First with the MA-X and AR (\"Acoustic Response\"), then the AD (\"Acoustic Dynamic\") and SA-X line in 2001 and 2002 respectively, then the MA (\"Metal Alloy\") line in 2004. The SA (\"Super Avilyn\") and D (\"Dynamic\") lines were withdrawn in 2011. Industry trends see the company moving into new forms of media. In 2004, TDK was the first media manufacturer to join the companies developing [[Blu-ray Disc|BD]] post-[[DVD]] technology.<ref name=\"corp history aae10000\">{{cite web|title=TDK Japan site: corporate history|url=http://www.tdk.co.jp/teaae01/aae10000.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170327015802/http://www.tdk.co.jp/teaae01/aae10000.htm|archive-date=2017-03-27}}</ref> \nTDK operated a [[Teridian Semiconductor|semiconductor division in California]] for about a decade, but divested it in 2005.\n\nThe company dabbled in the video game business in the late 1990s and early 2000s, by operating [[TDK Mediactive]] in the U.S. and Europe, and TDK Core in Japan.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gantayat |first=Anoop |date=2005-11-15 |title=Gaming Life in Japan |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2005/11/15/gaming-life-in-japan-65 |access-date=2023-09-26 |website=IGN |language=en}}</ref>\n\nIn late 2007, [[Imation]] acquired TDK's recording media business, including flash media, optical media, magnetic tape, and accessories, for $300 million.<ref name='reg_sale' /><ref name='tdk_sale_pr' /> This also included a license to use the \"TDK Life on Record\" brand on data storage and audio products<ref>{{cite web|quote=Imation acquired the recording media business of TDK Corporation (2007), with an exclusive license to the TDK Life on Record brand for data storage and audio products.|access-date=2015-12-17|url=https://www.imation.com/en/about-imation/history/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150910153547/https://www.imation.com/en/about-imation/history/|archive-date=2015-09-10|title=Imation Corp: Our History|publisher=Imation Corporation}}</ref> for 25 years.<ref name='reg_sale'>{{cite web|url=https://www.theregister.co.uk/2007/04/19/imation_buys_tdk_recording_media_business|quote=TDK is to sell its recording media business to Imation for $300m in stock and cash. [..] Under the agreement, Imation will use the TDK brand for present and future recordable magnetic, optical and flash media products worldwide. The right is revokable by TDK after 25 years.|date=2007-04-19|access-date=2015-12-17|website=The Register|author=Austin Modine|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150912010320/https://www.theregister.co.uk/2007/04/19/imation_buys_tdk_recording_media_business/|archive-date=2015-09-12|title=Imation buys TDK's recording media biz}}</ref> In September 2015, Imation announced that it had agreed to relinquish this license<ref>{{cite web|url=http://uk.reuters.com/article/mn-imation-corp-idUKnBw286666a+100+BSW20150928|title=Imation Announces Steps to Accelerate Strategic Transformation|quote=As part of its efforts to become a pure-play data storage and security company, Imation reached a definitive agreement with TDK under which [..] Imation’s license rights to the TDK Life on Record trademark will terminate|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151217232202/http://uk.reuters.com/article/mn-imation-corp-idUKnBw286666a+100+BSW20150928|archive-date=2015-12-17|date=2015-09-28|type=Press release|url-status=dead|publisher=Imation via Reuters}}</ref> and would cease selling TDK-branded products by the end of the year.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tdkperformance.com/redirect/|archive-url=https://archive.today/20151211004724/http://www.tdkperformance.com/redirect/|quote=Effective December 31, 2015, Imation Corp. will no longer be selling products licensed under the TDK Life on Record brand.|title=TDK Life on Record|access-date=2015-12-17|archive-date=2015-12-11}}.</ref>\n\nSince the 2000s, TDK has focused on the development, manufacture and sales of electronic components, HDD heads and suspension, and power supplies.\n\nBeginning in 2005, TDK has acquired many types of electronic device manufacturers including passive component developers, sensors manufacturers and power supply companies. These areas remain TDK's focus today.<ref name=\"tdk our history\"/>\n\nSince acquiring numerous companies and sharpening its product line focus in recent years, TDK has begun building a portfolio of varying sensors, actuators and power electronic components through these brands. These include multi-axis [[MEMS]] motion tracking devices and MEMS microphones from [[InvenSense]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.invensense.com/products/motion-tracking/9-axis/icm-20948/|title=Icm-20948 &#124; TDK}}</ref> a point-of-load DC-DC converter from Faraday Semi,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.tdk.com/corp/en/news_center/press/20190318_01.htm|title=Power Management Products: World's smallest* Point-Of-Load DC-DC converter {{!}} Press Releases {{!}} News Center {{!}}TDK GLOBAL|date=18 March 2019|website=TDK|language=en|access-date=2020-02-16}}</ref> and MEMS-based ultrasonic Time-of-Flight sensors from Chirp Microsystems intended for [[consumer electronics]], AR/VR, robotics, drones, IoT, automotive and industrial market segments.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.microcontrollertips.com/chirp-ch-101-tof-sensor-ultrasonic-transducer-dsp-asic/|title=Time-of-Flight sensor combines ultrasonic transducer with DSP on mixed-signal ASIC|last=Kalnoskas|first=Aimee|date=2019-06-25|website=Electrical Engineering News and Products|language=en-US|access-date=2020-02-16}}</ref> Other areas of TDK's recent focus include power components for mobile devices,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.tdk.com/corp/en/news_center/press/20190528_01.htm|title=Inductors: High efficiency thin-film power inductors for mobile devices {{!}} Press Releases {{!}} News Center {{!}}TDK GLOBAL|website=TDK|language=en|access-date=2020-02-16}}</ref> high-stability MEMS accelerometers from Tronics,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.tronics.tdk.com/inertial-sensors/high-performance-mems-inertial-sensors/axo-high-performance-mems-accelerometers/|title=TDK Tronics MEMS Accelerometers|website=Tronic’s Microsytems|url-status=live}}</ref> miniaturized haptic actuators,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2019/05/14/1823129/0/en/TDK-Extends-Portfolio-of-Mini-PowerHap-Haptic-Feedback-Actuators.html|title=TDK Extends Portfolio of Mini PowerHap Haptic Feedback Actuators|last=TDK|date=2019-05-14|website=[[GlobeNewswire]] News Room|access-date=2020-02-16}}</ref> and custom ASICs (Application Specific ICs) through its subsidiary ICsense.\n\nIn 2017, TDK and [[GenCell]] began collaborating to develop and produce an [[Ammonia#As a fuel|ammonia-based fuel]] solution.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Kelly-Detwiler|first=Peter|title=A Key To The 'Hydrogen Economy' Is Carbon-Free Ammonia|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/peterdetwiler/2020/12/16/maybe-the-hydrogen-economy-will-become-the-ammonia-economy/|access-date=2021-04-09|website=Forbes|language=en}}</ref>\n\nOn 29 September 2026, TDK signed a memorandum of understanding with [[Taiyo Yuden]] to explore joint development of multilayer ceramic capacitors and inductors, together with cooperation in manufacturing and procurement.<ref>{{cite web |title=TDK and TAIYO YUDEN enter into a memorandum of understanding to explore business alliance |url=https://www.tdk.com/en/news_center/press/20260929_01.html |publisher=TDK Corporation |date=2026-09-29 |access-date=2026-09-30}}</ref> The agreement initiated discussions on the scope and responsibilities of a proposed business alliance, including the possibility of a future capital alliance.<ref>{{cite news |author=Tokyo Brief Desk |title=Taiyo Yuden and TDK sign basic agreement to study joint MLCC and inductor development |url=https://tokyobrief.com/articles/taiyo-yuden-tdk-basic-agreement-mlcc-inductor-alliance |work=Tokyo Brief |date=2026-09-29 |access-date=2026-09-30}}</ref>\n\n==Key acquisitions and joint ventures==\n*1986:\tSAE Magnetics (H.K.) Ltd., a magnetic head maker based in Hong Kong<ref name=\"TDK company profile 2018\">{{cite web|title=company_profile_2018_e_00.pdf|url=https://www.global.tdk.com/news_center/publications/tdk_company_profile/pdf/company_profile_2018_e_00.pdf}}</ref>\n*2000:\tHeadway Technologies, a magnetic head maker based in the United States<ref name=\"TDK company profile 2018\"/>\n*2005:\tAmperex Technology Limited, a Lithium Polymer battery company based in Hong Kong<ref name=\"TDK company profile 2018\"/>\n*2005:\tLambda Power Division, a group of power supply businesses of London-based Invensys PLC.<ref name=\"TDK acquires Lambda\">{{cite web|title=TDK to acquire Lambda Power Division from Invensys|url=https://www.eetimes.com/document.asp?doc_id=1155293|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181128075707/https://www.eetimes.com/document.asp?doc_id=1155293|archive-date=2018-11-28}}</ref>\n*2008:\t[[Epcos]], an electronic device manufacturer based in Germany<ref name=\"TDK acquires EPCOS\">{{cite web|title=TDK to Acquire Germany's Epcos|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB121749648541600349|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181128075346/https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB121749648541600349|archive-date=2018-11-28}}</ref>\n*2016:\tHutchinson Technology Inc., a manufacturer of HDD suspension assemblies based in the United States<ref name=\"TDK acquires Hutchinson\">{{cite web|title=TDK to buy Hutchinson Technology for $126 million|website=[[Star Tribune]]|url=http://www.startribune.com/tdk-buys-hutchinson-technology-for-126-million/339332671/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180701083538/http://www.startribune.com/tdk-buys-hutchinson-technology-for-126-million/339332671/|archive-date=2018-07-01}}</ref>\n*2016:\tMicronas Semiconductor Holding AG<ref name=\"TDK Micronas\">{{cite web|title=TDK will sich Micronas einverleiben|url=https://www.handelszeitung.ch/unternehmen/tdk-will-sich-micronas-einverleiben-945326}}</ref>\n*2017:\tRF360 Holdings Singapore PTE Ltd. – a joint venture with [[Qualcomm|Qualcomm Inc.]] (USA)<ref name=\"TDK acquires RF360\">{{cite web|title=Qualcomm, TDK Prep $3bn RF Joint Venture|url=https://www.eetimes.com/document.asp?doc_id=1328683|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181128075645/https://www.eetimes.com/document.asp?doc_id=1328683|archive-date=2018-11-28}}</ref>\n*2017:\t[[ICsense]] NV, a mixed-signal ASIC design & supply company based in Belgium<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.icsense.com/icsense-becomes-part-of-tdk-group/|title=ICsense becomes part of TDK group|date=2017-03-28}}</ref>\n*2017:\tInvenSense, Inc., a sensor specialist based in the United States<ref name=\"TDK acquires InvenSense\">{{cite web|title=TDK Agrees to Buy InvenSense for About $1.3 Billion in Cash|website=[[Bloomberg News]]|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-21/tdk-agrees-to-buy-invensense-for-about-1-3-billion-in-cash|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181120124014/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-21/tdk-agrees-to-buy-invensense-for-about-1-3-billion-in-cash|archive-date=2018-11-20}}</ref>\n*2018:\tChirp Microsystems, a developer of low power, ultrasonic 3D-sensing solutions based in the United States<ref name=\"TDK acquires Chirp Microsystems\">{{cite web|title=Chirp Slurped up by TDK|url=https://www.eetimes.com/document.asp?doc_id=1333018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181128075602/https://www.eetimes.com/document.asp?doc_id=1333018|archive-date=2018-11-28}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=TDK to acquire Chirp Microsystems, aiming for leadership in ultrasonic sensing solutions |url=https://invensense.tdk.com/news-media/tdk-to-acquire-chirp-microsystems-aiming-for-leadership-in-ultrasonic-sensing-solutions/ |access-date=2025-06-11 |website=TDK InvenSense |language=en-US}}</ref>\n*2018:\tFaraday Semi LLC, a developer of miniature Point of Load (PoL) solutions based in the United States<ref name=\"TDK acquires Faraday Semi\">{{cite web|title=TDK buys Faraday Semi for power chip and 3D packaging technology|date=9 May 2018 |url=http://www.eenewspower.com/news/tdk-buys-faraday-semi-power-chip-and-3d-packaging-technology|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181128075642/http://www.eenewspower.com/news/tdk-buys-faraday-semi-power-chip-and-3d-packaging-technology|archive-date=2018-11-28}}</ref>\n*2023:\tQeexo, co. a developer of embedded AI / TinyML technologies based in the United States<ref>{{Cite web |date=4 January 2023 |title=TDK to acquire Qeexo to enable complete smart edge platforms |url=https://www.tdk.com/en/news_center/press/20230104_01.html |access-date=17 October 2023 |website=TDK}}</ref>\n\n==Sponsorship and advertising==\n[[File:Close up of TDK sign at Piccadilly Circus January 2012.jpg|thumb|The former TDK sign at [[Piccadilly Circus]] in [[London]]]]\nTDK has sponsored the IAAF World Championships in Athletics since the 1983 inaugural event in Helsinki.<ref name=\"IAAF World Championships in Athletics\">{{cite web|title=IAAF World Championships in Athletics|url=https://www.global.tdk.com/corp/en/about_tdk/corporate_branding/athletic.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181129100059/https://www.global.tdk.com/corp/en/about_tdk/corporate_branding/athletic.htm|archive-date=2018-11-29|publisher=TDK Corporation}}</ref>\n\nTDK sponsored [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] for several years in the 1980s during which it won [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]] in [[1986–87 European Cup Winners' Cup|1987]]. From 1993 to 1999, TDK were also the sponsors of the English football club [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]], who were promoted to the [[Premier League]] twice during this era, though lasting for just one season before being relegated on both occasions. TDK was also a minor sponsor of the [[Brisbane Broncos]] Rugby League Team during the early 1990s. It also sponsors activities and events such as those at The Cross [[nightclub]] in [[Central London]], and it had a prominent sign at [[Piccadilly Circus]] from 1990. The contract for this sign was terminated in 2015, as TDK was moving away from consumer electronics.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Monkey|url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2015/mar/11/tdk-ad-london-piccadilly-circus|title=TDK ad at Piccadilly Circus: lights go out on 25 years of history|date=2015-03-11|work=[[The Guardian]]|access-date=2020-02-16|language=en-GB|issn=0261-3077}}</ref>\n\nTDK has owned a sign on [[One Times Square]] since 2000. The screen is placed under that of [[Toshiba]] and can be seen during the annual Times Square New Year's [[Times Square Ball|Ball Drop]].<ref name=\"TDK Corporate Branding Activities\">{{cite web|title=Outdoor Advertising|url=https://www.global.tdk.com/corp/en/about_tdk/corporate_branding/index.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181129100126/https://www.global.tdk.com/corp/en/about_tdk/corporate_branding/index.htm|archive-date=2018-11-29|publisher=TDK Corporation}}</ref>\n\nSince 2001, TDK has supported performances of some of the world's distinguished orchestras in Japan within the company's \"TDK Orchestra Concerts\" program. TDK's \"Outreach-Mini Concerts\" and \"Special Rehearsals and Main Concert Invitations\" additionally serve as avenues for the company to attract younger audiences.<ref name=\"TDK Orchestra Concerts\">{{cite web|title=TDK Orchestra Concerts|url=https://www.global.tdk.com/corp/en/about_tdk/corporate_branding/concert.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181129101617/https://www.global.tdk.com/corp/en/about_tdk/corporate_branding/concert.htm|archive-date=2018-11-29|publisher=TDK Corporation}}</ref>\n\nIn October 2024, [[McLaren]] announced TDK as the official technology partner for the [[McLaren Formula E Team]] and the McLaren Shadow F1 Sim Racing Team.<ref>{{Cite web |date=29 October 2024 |title=McLaren Racing announces TDK as an Official Partner of the NEOM McLaren Formula E Team and McLaren Shadow F1 Sim Racing Team |url=https://www.mclaren.com/racing/partners/tdk/mclaren-racing-announces-tdk-as-an-official-partner/ |access-date=29 October 2024 |website=[[McLaren]] |language=en-GB}}</ref>\n\n[[File:Ajax tegen Feyenoord 1-3 ereronde Ajax met Europa Cup I v.l.n.r. Scholten, Silo, Bestanddeelnr 933-9819.jpg|thumb|right|Ajax players in 1987]]\n\n==Museum==\n[[File:TDK Museum.jpg|thumb|right|TDK Museum]]\nTDK operates a company museum in Nikaho, Akita, Japan. The museum is open to the public, free of charge. Among its exhibits are a comprehensive history of the company, its products and technologies, and emerging developments.<ref name=\"TDK Museum\">{{cite web|title=About the TDK Museum|url=https://www.tdk.co.jp/museum/en/about/|publisher=TDK Corporation|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181129100058/https://www.tdk.co.jp/museum/en/about/|archive-date=2018-11-29}}</ref>\n\n==TDK products==\n<gallery>\nFile:TDK D60 1979.JPG|D60 audio cassette from 1979\nFile:TDK MA60 001.jpg|MA60 - 1990-92\nFile:TDK D C180 cassette.jpg|D-C180 - three hour audio cassette \nFile:VHS-C 02.JPG|[[VHS-C]] video cassettes\nFile:TDK blank DVD.JPG|Blank DVD\nFile:Minidisk01.jpg|[[MiniDisc]]\nFile:TDK DC4-150-9262.jpg|DDS-3 data cartridge\nFile:A VHS TDK HS cassette.jpg|TDK HS VHS cassette\n</gallery>\n\n==References==\n<references>\n\n<ref name='tdk_sale_pr'>{{cite press release|access-date=2026-09-12|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221205112716/https://www.tdk.com/en/news_center/press/aah21400.html|archivedate=2022-12-05|url=https://www.tdk.com/en/news_center/press/aah21400.html|title=Imation and TDK Agree to Imation's Acquisition of TDK Brand Recording Media Business for $300 Million in Stock and Cash|website=tdk.com|date=2007-04-19}}</ref>\n\n</references>\n\n==External links==\n{{Commons category|TDK}}\n* {{Official website|https://www.tdk.com/ }}\n* [http://www.global.tdk.com/ Official Global Website]\n* [https://www.us.tdk.com/ TDK US Website]\n* [https://in.tdk-electronics.tdk.com/ TDK India Website]\n* [https://www.tdk.com/museum/en/ The TDK history museum]\n\n{{Japanese Electronics Industry}}\n{{Nikkei 225}}\n{{Authority control}}\n\n{{DEFAULTSORT:Tdk}}\n[[Category:TDK| ]]\n[[Category:Engineering companies based in Tokyo]]\n[[Category:Manufacturing companies based in Tokyo]]\n[[Category:Nihonbashi]]\n[[Category:Electronics companies established in 1935]]\n[[Category:Companies formerly listed on the London Stock Exchange]]\n[[Category:Companies listed on the Tokyo Stock Exchange]]\n[[Category:Companies in the Nikkei 225]]\n[[Category:Electronics companies of Japan]]\n[[Category:Japanese brands]]\n[[Category:Defense companies of Japan]]\n[[Category:Capacitor manufacturers]]\n[[Category:Japanese companies established in 1935]]\n[[Category:1960s initial public offerings]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-10-30T03:57:24Z","lastrevid":1381749944,"length":24796}]}}